<body>
<p align=3D"CENTER">
<b>BERKSHIRE HATHAWAY INC.</b>
</p>

<pre>
                                                  March 3, 1983



<i>To the Stockholders of Berkshire Hathaway Inc.:</i>

     Operating earnings of $31.5 million in 1982 amounted to only=20
9.8% of beginning equity capital (valuing securities at cost),=20
down from 15.2% in 1981 and far below our recent high of 19.4% in=20
1978.  This decline largely resulted from:

     (1) a significant deterioration in insurance underwriting=20
         results;

     (2) a considerable expansion of equity capital without a=20
         corresponding growth in the businesses we operate=20
         directly; and

     (3) a continually-enlarging commitment of our resources to=20
         investment in partially-owned, nonoperated businesses;=20
         accounting rules dictate that a major part of our=20
         pro-rata share of earnings from such businesses must be=20
         excluded from Berkshire=92s reported earnings.

     It was only a few years ago that we told you that the=20
operating earnings/equity capital percentage, with proper=20
allowance for a few other variables, was the most important=20
yardstick of single-year managerial performance.  While we still=20
believe this to be the case with the vast majority of companies,=20
we believe its utility in our own case has greatly diminished. =20
You should be suspicious of such an assertion.  Yardsticks seldom=20
are discarded while yielding favorable readings.  But when=20
results deteriorate, most managers favor disposition of the=20
yardstick rather than disposition of the manager.

     To managers faced with such deterioration, a more flexible=20
measurement system often suggests itself: just shoot the arrow of=20
business performance into a blank canvas and then carefully draw=20
the bullseye around the implanted arrow.  We generally believe in=20
pre-set, long-lived and small bullseyes.  However, because of the=20
importance of item (3) above, further explained in the following=20
section, we believe our abandonment of the operating=20
earnings/equity capital bullseye to be warranted.


<i>Non-Reported Ownership Earnings</i>

     The appended financial statements reflect =93accounting=94=20
earnings that generally include our proportionate share of=20
earnings from any underlying business in which our ownership is=20
at least 20%.  Below the 20% ownership figure, however, only our=20
share of dividends paid by the underlying business units is=20
included in our accounting numbers; undistributed earnings of=20
such less-than-20%-owned businesses are totally ignored.

     There are a few exceptions to this rule; e.g., we own about=20
35% of GEICO Corporation but, because we have assigned our voting=20
rights, the company is treated for accounting purposes as a less-
than-20% holding.  Thus, dividends received from GEICO in 1982 of=20
$3.5 million after tax are the only item included in our=20
=93accounting=94earnings.  An additional $23 million that represents=20
our share of GEICO=92s undistributed operating earnings for 1982 is=20
totally excluded from our reported operating earnings.  If GEICO=20
had earned less money in 1982 but had paid an additional $1=20
million in dividends, our reported earnings would have been=20
larger despite the poorer business results.  Conversely, if GEICO=20
had earned an additional $100 million - and retained it all - our=20
reported earnings would have been unchanged.  Clearly=20
=93accounting=94 earnings can seriously misrepresent economic=20
reality.

     We prefer a concept of =93economic=94 earnings that includes all=20
undistributed earnings, regardless of ownership percentage.  In=20
our view, the value to all owners of the retained earnings of a=20
business enterprise is determined by the effectiveness with which=20
those earnings are used - and not by the size of one=92s ownership=20
percentage.  If you have owned .01 of 1% of Berkshire during the=20
past decade, you have benefited economically in full measure from=20
your share of our retained earnings, no matter what your=20
accounting system.  Proportionately, you have done just as well=20
as if you had owned the magic 20%.  But if you have owned 100% of=20
a great many capital-intensive businesses during the decade,=20
retained earnings that were credited fully and with painstaking=20
precision to you under standard accounting methods have resulted=20
in minor or zero economic value.  This is not a criticism of=20
accounting procedures.  We would not like to have the job of=20
designing a better system.  It=92s simply to say that managers and=20
investors alike must understand that accounting numbers are the=20
beginning, not the end, of business valuation.

     In most corporations, less-than-20% ownership positions are=20
unimportant (perhaps, in part, because they prevent maximization=20
of cherished reported earnings) and the distinction between=20
accounting and economic results we have just discussed matters=20
little.  But in our own case, such positions are of very large=20
and growing importance.  Their magnitude, we believe, is what=20
makes our reported operating earnings figure of limited=20
significance.

     In our 1981 annual report we predicted that our share of=20
undistributed earnings from four of our major non-controlled=20
holdings would aggregate over $35 million in 1982.  With no=20
change in our holdings of three of these companies - GEICO,=20
General Foods and The Washington Post - and a considerable=20
increase in our ownership of the fourth, R. J. Reynolds=20
Industries, our share of undistributed 1982 operating earnings of=20
this group came to well over $40 million.  This number - not=20
reflected at all in our earnings - is greater than our total=20
reported earnings, which include only the $14 million in=20
dividends received from these companies.  And, of course, we have=20
a number of smaller ownership interests that, in aggregate, had=20
substantial additional undistributed earnings.

      We attach real significance to the general magnitude of=20
these numbers, but we don=92t believe they should be carried to ten=20
decimal places.  Realization by Berkshire of such retained=20
earnings through improved market valuations is subject to very=20
substantial, but indeterminate, taxation.  And while retained=20
earnings over the years, and in the aggregate, have translated=20
into at least equal market value for shareholders, the=20
translation has been both extraordinarily uneven among companies=20
and irregular and unpredictable in timing.

     However, this very unevenness and irregularity offers=20
advantages to the value-oriented purchaser of fractional portions=20
of businesses.  This investor may select from almost the entire=20
array of major American corporations, including many far superior=20
to virtually any of the businesses that could be bought in their=20
entirety in a negotiated deal.  And fractional-interest purchases=20
can be made in an auction market where prices are set by=20
participants with behavior patterns that sometimes resemble those=20
of an army of manic-depressive lemmings.

     Within this gigantic auction arena, it is our job to select=20
businesses with economic characteristics allowing each dollar of=20
retained earnings to be translated eventually into at least a=20
dollar of market value.  Despite a lot of mistakes, we have so=20
far achieved this goal.  In doing so, we have been greatly=20
assisted by Arthur Okun=92s patron saint for economists - St.=20
Offset.  In some cases, that is, retained earnings attributable=20
to our ownership position have had insignificant or even negative=20
impact on market value, while in other major positions a dollar=20
retained by an investee corporation has been translated into two=20
or more dollars of market value.  To date, our corporate over-
achievers have more than offset the laggards.  If we can continue=20
this record, it will validate our efforts to maximize =93economic=94=20
earnings, regardless of the impact upon =93accounting=94 earnings.

     Satisfactory as our partial-ownership approach has been,=20
what really makes us dance is the purchase of 100% of good=20
businesses at reasonable prices.  We=92ve accomplished this feat a=20
few times (and expect to do so again), but it is an=20
extraordinarily difficult job - far more difficult than the=20
purchase at attractive prices of fractional interests.

     As we look at the major acquisitions that others made during=20
1982, our reaction is not envy, but relief that we were non-
participants.  For in many of these acquisitions, managerial=20
intellect wilted in competition with managerial adrenaline The=20
thrill of the chase blinded the pursuers to the consequences of=20
the catch.  Pascal=92s observation seems apt: =93It has struck me=20
that all men=92s misfortunes spring from the single cause that they=20
are unable to stay quietly in one room.=94

     (Your Chairman left the room once too often last year and=20
almost starred in the Acquisition Follies of 1982.  In=20
retrospect, our major accomplishment of the year was that a very=20
large purchase to which we had firmly committed was unable to be=20
completed for reasons totally beyond our control.  Had it come=20
off, this transaction would have consumed extraordinary amounts=20
of time and energy, all for a most uncertain payoff.  If we were=20
to introduce graphics to this report, illustrating favorable=20
business developments of the past year, two blank pages depicting=20
this blown deal would be the appropriate centerfold.)

     Our partial-ownership approach can be continued soundly only=20
as long as portions of attractive businesses can be acquired at=20
attractive prices.  We need a moderately-priced stock market to=20
assist us in this endeavor.  The market, like the Lord, helps=20
those who help themselves.  But, unlike the Lord, the market does=20
not forgive those who know not what they do.  For the investor, a=20
too-high purchase price for the stock of an excellent company can=20
undo the effects of a subsequent decade of favorable business=20
developments.

     Should the stock market advance to considerably higher=20
levels, our ability to utilize capital effectively in partial-
ownership positions will be reduced or eliminated.  This will=20
happen periodically: just ten years ago, at the height of the=20
two-tier market mania (with high-return-on-equity businesses bid=20
to the sky by institutional investors), Berkshire=92s insurance=20
subsidiaries owned only $18 million in market value of equities,=20
excluding their interest in Blue Chip Stamps.  At that time, such=20
equity holdings amounted to about 15% of our insurance company=20
investments versus the present 80%.  There were as many good=20
businesses around in 1972 as in 1982, but the prices the stock=20
market placed upon those businesses in 1972 looked absurd.  While=20
high stock prices in the future would make our performance look=20
good temporarily, they would hurt our long-term business=20
prospects rather than help them.  We currently are seeing early=20
traces of this problem.


<i>Long-Term Corporate Performance</i>

     Our gain in net worth during 1982, valuing equities held by=20
our insurance subsidiaries at market value (less capital gain=20
taxes payable if unrealized gains were actually realized)=20
amounted to $208 million.  On a beginning net worth base of $519=20
million, the percentage gain was 40%.

     During the 18-year tenure of present management, book value=20
has grown from $19.46 per share to $737.43 per share, or 22.0%=20
compounded annually.  You can be certain that this percentage=20
will diminish in the future.  Geometric progressions eventually=20
forge their own anchors.

     Berkshire=92s economic goal remains to produce a long-term=20
rate of return well above the return achieved by the average=20
large American corporation.  Our willingness to purchase either=20
partial or total ownership positions in favorably-situated=20
businesses, coupled with reasonable discipline about the prices=20
we are willing to pay, should give us a good chance of achieving=20
our goal.

     Again this year the gain in market valuation of partially-
owned businesses outpaced the gain in underlying economic value=20
of those businesses.  For example, $79 million of our $208=20
million gain is attributable to an increased market price for=20
GEICO.  This company continues to do exceptionally well, and we=20
are more impressed than ever by the strength of GEICO=92s basic=20
business idea and by the management skills of Jack Byrne.=20
(Although not found in the catechism of the better business=20
schools, =93Let Jack Do It=94 works fine as a corporate creed for=20
us.)

     However, GEICO=92s increase in market value during the past=20
two years has been considerably greater than the gain in its=20
intrinsic business value, impressive as the latter has been.  We=20
expected such a favorable variation at some point, as the=20
perception of investors converged with business reality.  And we=20
look forward to substantial future gains in underlying business=20
value accompanied by irregular, but eventually full, market=20
recognition of such gains.

     Year-to-year variances, however, cannot consistently be in=20
our favor.  Even if our partially-owned businesses continue to=20
perform well in an economic sense, there will be years when they=20
perform poorly in the market.  At such times our net worth could=20
shrink significantly.  We will not be distressed by such a=20
shrinkage; if the businesses continue to look attractive and we=20
have cash available, we simply will add to our holdings at even=20
more favorable prices.


