     The Best=92s data reflect the experience of practically the=20
entire industry, including stock, mutual and reciprocal=20
companies.  The combined ratio represents total operating and=20
loss costs as compared to revenue from premiums; a ratio below=20
100 indicates an underwriting profit, and one above 100 indicates=20
a loss.

     For reasons outlined in last year=92s report, as long as the=20
annual gain in industry premiums written falls well below 10%,=20
you can expect the underwriting picture in the next year to=20
deteriorate.  This will be true even at today=92s lower general=20
rate of inflation.  With the number of policies increasing=20
annually, medical inflation far exceeding general inflation, and=20
concepts of insured liability broadening, it is highly unlikely=20
that yearly increases in insured losses will fall much below 10%. =20

     You should be further aware that the 1982 combined ratio of=20
109.5 represents a =93best case=94 estimate.  In a given year, it is=20
possible for an insurer to show almost any profit number it=20
wishes, particularly if it (1) writes =93long-tail=94 business=20
(coverage where current costs can be only estimated, because=20
claim payments are long delayed), (2) has been adequately=20
reserved in the past, or (3)	is growing very rapidly.  There are=20
indications that several large insurers opted in 1982 for obscure=20
accounting and reserving maneuvers that masked significant=20
deterioration in their underlying businesses.  In insurance, as=20
elsewhere, the reaction of weak managements to weak operations is=20
often weak accounting. (=93It=92s difficult for an empty sack to=20
stand upright.=94)

     The great majority of managements, however, try to play it=20
straight.  But even managements of integrity may subconsciously=20
be less willing in poor profit years to fully recognize adverse=20
loss trends.  Industry statistics indicate some deterioration in=20
loss reserving practices during 1982 and the true combined ratio=20
is likely to be modestly worse than indicated by our table.

     The conventional wisdom is that 1983 or 1984 will see the=20
worst of underwriting experience and then, as in the past, the=20
=93cycle=94 will move, significantly and steadily, toward better=20
results.  We disagree because of a pronounced change in the=20
competitive environment, hard to see for many years but now quite=20
visible.

     To understand the change, we need to look at some major=20
factors that affect levels of corporate profitability generally. =20
Businesses in industries with both substantial over-capacity and=20
a =93commodity=94 product (undifferentiated in any customer-important=20
way by factors such as performance, appearance, service support,=20
etc.) are prime candidates for profit troubles.  These may be=20
escaped, true, if prices or costs are administered in some manner=20
and thereby insulated at least partially from normal market=20
forces.  This administration can be carried out (a) legally=20
through government intervention (until recently, this category=20
included pricing for truckers and deposit costs for financial=20
institutions), (b) illegally through collusion, or (c) =93extra-
legally=94 through OPEC-style foreign cartelization (with tag-along=20
benefits for domestic non-cartel operators).

     If, however, costs and prices are determined by full-bore=20
competition, there is more than ample capacity, and the buyer=20
cares little about whose product or distribution services he=20
uses, industry economics are almost certain to be unexciting. =20
They may well be disastrous.

     Hence the constant struggle of every vendor to establish and=20
emphasize special qualities of product or service.  This works=20
with candy bars (customers buy by brand name, not by asking for a=20
=93two-ounce candy bar=94) but doesn=92t work with sugar (how often do=20
you hear, =93I=92ll have a cup of coffee with cream and C &amp; H sugar,=20
please=94).

     In many industries, differentiation simply can=92t be made=20
meaningful.  A few producers in such industries may consistently=20
do well if they have a cost advantage that is both wide and=20
sustainable.  By definition such exceptions are few, and, in many=20
industries, are non-existent.  For the great majority of=20
companies selling =93commodity=94products, a depressing equation of=20
business economics prevails: persistent over-capacity without=20
administered prices (or costs) equals poor profitability.

     Of course, over-capacity may eventually self-correct, either=20
as capacity shrinks or demand expands.  Unfortunately for the=20
participants, such corrections often are long delayed.  When they=20
finally occur, the rebound to prosperity frequently produces a=20
pervasive enthusiasm for expansion that, within a few years,=20
again creates over-capacity and a new profitless environment.  In=20
other words, nothing fails like success.

     What finally determines levels of long-term profitability in=20
such industries is the ratio of supply-tight to supply-ample=20
years.  Frequently that ratio is dismal. (It seems as if the most=20
recent supply-tight period in our textile business - it occurred=20
some years back - lasted the better part of a morning.)

     In some industries, however, capacity-tight conditions can=20
last a long time.  Sometimes actual growth in demand will outrun=20
forecasted growth for an extended period.  In other cases, adding=20
capacity requires very long lead times because complicated=20
manufacturing facilities must be planned and built.

     But in the insurance business, to return to that subject,=20
capacity can be instantly created by capital plus an=20
underwriter=92s willingness to sign his name. (Even capital is less=20
important in a world in which state-sponsored guaranty funds=20
protect many policyholders against insurer insolvency.) Under=20
almost all conditions except that of fear for survival -=20
produced, perhaps, by a stock market debacle or a truly major=20
natural disaster - the insurance industry operates under the=20
competitive sword of substantial overcapacity.  Generally, also,=20
despite heroic attempts to do otherwise, the industry sells a=20
relatively undifferentiated commodity-type product. (Many=20
insureds, including the managers of large businesses, do not even=20
know the names of their insurers.) Insurance, therefore, would=20
seem to be a textbook case of an industry usually faced with the=20
deadly combination of excess capacity and a =93commodity=94 product.

     Why, then, was underwriting, despite the existence of=20
cycles, generally profitable over many decades? (From 1950=20
through 1970, the industry combined ratio averaged 99.0. =20
allowing all investment income plus 1% of premiums to flow=20
through to profits.) The answer lies primarily in the historic=20
methods of regulation and distribution.  For much of this=20
century, a large portion of the industry worked, in effect,=20
within a legal quasi-administered pricing system fostered by=20
insurance regulators.  While price competition existed, it was=20
not pervasive among the larger companies.  The main competition=20
was for agents, who were courted via various non-price-related=20
strategies.

     For the giants of the industry, most rates were set through=20
negotiations between industry =93bureaus=94 (or through companies=20
acting in accord with their recommendations) and state=20
regulators.  Dignified haggling occurred, but it was between=20
company and regulator rather than between company and customer. =20
When the dust settled, Giant A charged the same price as Giant B=20
- and both companies and agents were prohibited by law from=20
cutting such filed rates.

     The company-state negotiated prices included specific profit=20
allowances and, when loss data indicated that current prices were=20
unprofitable, both company managements and state regulators=20
expected that they would act together to correct the situation. =20
Thus, most of the pricing actions of the giants of the industry=20
were =93gentlemanly=94, predictable, and profit-producing.  Of prime=20
importance - and in contrast to the way most of the business=20
world operated - insurance companies could legally price their=20
way to profitability even in the face of substantial over-
capacity.

     That day is gone.  Although parts of the old structure=20
remain, far more than enough new capacity exists outside of that=20
structure to force all parties, old and new, to respond.  The new=20
capacity uses various methods of distribution and is not=20
reluctant to use price as a prime competitive weapon.  Indeed, it=20
relishes that use.  In the process, customers have learned that=20
insurance is no longer a one-price business.  They won=92t forget.

     Future profitability of the industry will be determined by=20
current competitive characteristics, not past ones.  Many=20
managers have been slow to recognize this.  It=92s not only=20
generals that prefer to fight the last war.  Most business and=20
investment analysis also comes from the rear-view mirror.  It=20
seems clear to us, however, that only one condition will allow=20
the insurance industry to achieve significantly improved=20
underwriting results.  That is the same condition that will allow=20
better results for the aluminum, copper, or corn producer - a=20
major narrowing of the gap between demand and supply.

     Unfortunately, there can be no surge in demand for insurance=20
policies comparable to one that might produce a market tightness=20
in copper or aluminum.  Rather, the supply of available insurance=20
coverage must be curtailed.  =93Supply=94, in this context, is mental=20
rather than physical: plants or companies need not be shut; only=20
the willingness of underwriters to sign their names need be=20
curtailed.

     This contraction will not happen because of generally poor=20
profit levels.  Bad profits produce much hand-wringing and=20
finger-pointing.  But they do not lead major sources of insurance=20
capacity to turn their backs on very large chunks of business,=20
thereby sacrificing market share and industry significance.

     Instead, major capacity withdrawals require a shock factor=20
such as a natural or financial =93megadisaster=94.  One might occur=20
tomorrow - or many years from now.  The insurance business - even=20
taking investment income into account - will not be particularly=20
profitable in the meantime.

     When supply ultimately contracts, large amounts of business=20
will be available for the few with large capital capacity, a=20
willingness to commit it, and an in-place distribution system. =20
We would expect great opportunities for our insurance=20
subsidiaries at such a time.

     During 1982, our insurance underwriting deteriorated far=20
more than did the industry=92s.  From a profit position well above=20
average, we, slipped to a performance modestly below average. =20
The biggest swing was in National Indemnity=92s traditional=20
coverages.  Lines that have been highly profitable for us in the=20
past are now priced at levels that guarantee underwriting losses. =20
In 1983 we expect our insurance group to record an average=20
performance in an industry in which average is very poor.

     Two of our stars, Milt Thornton at Cypress and Floyd Taylor=20
at Kansas Fire and Casualty, continued their outstanding records=20
of producing an underwriting profit every year since joining us. =20
Both Milt and Floyd simply are incapable of being average.  They=20
maintain a passionately proprietary attitude toward their=20
operations and have developed a business culture centered upon=20
unusual cost-consciousness and customer service.  It shows on=20
their scorecards.

     During 1982, parent company responsibility for most of our=20
insurance operations was given to Mike Goldberg.  Planning,=20
recruitment, and monitoring all have shown significant=20
improvement since Mike replaced me in this role.

     GEICO continues to be managed with a zeal for efficiency and=20
value to the customer that virtually guarantees unusual success. =20
Jack Byrne and Bill Snyder are achieving the most elusive of=20
human goals - keeping things simple and remembering what you set=20
out to do.  In Lou Simpson, additionally, GEICO has the best=20
investment manager in the property-casualty business.  We are=20
happy with every aspect of this operation.  GEICO is a=20
magnificent illustration of the high-profit exception we=20
described earlier in discussing commodity industries with over-
capacity - a company with a wide and sustainable cost advantage. =20
Our 35% interest in GEICO represents about $250 million of=20
premium volume, an amount considerably greater than all of the=20
direct volume we produce.


<i>Issuance of Equity</i>

     Berkshire and Blue Chip are considering merger in 1983.  If=20
it takes place, it will involve an exchange of stock based upon=20
an identical valuation method applied to both companies.  The one=20
other significant issuance of shares by Berkshire or its=20
affiliated companies that occurred during present management=92s=20
tenure was in the 1978 merger of Berkshire with Diversified=20
Retailing Company.

     Our share issuances follow a simple basic rule: we will not=20
issue shares unless we receive as much intrinsic business value=20
as we give.  Such a policy might seem axiomatic.  Why, you might=20
ask, would anyone issue dollar bills in exchange for fifty-cent=20
pieces?  Unfortunately, many corporate managers have been willing=20
to do just that.

     The first choice of these managers in making acquisitions=20
may be to use cash or debt.  But frequently the CEO=92s cravings=20
outpace cash and credit resources (certainly mine always have). =20
Frequently, also, these cravings occur when his own stock is=20
selling far below intrinsic business value.  This state of=20
affairs produces a moment of truth.  At that point, as Yogi Berra=20
has said, =93You can observe a lot just by watching.=94 For=20
shareholders then will find which objective the management truly=20
prefers - expansion of domain or maintenance of owners=92 wealth.

     The need to choose between these objectives occurs for some=20
simple reasons.  Companies often sell in the stock market below=20
their intrinsic business value.  But when a company wishes to=20
sell out completely, in a negotiated transaction, it inevitably=20
wants to - and usually can - receive full business value in=20
whatever kind of currency the value is to be delivered.  If cash=20
is to be used in payment, the seller=92s calculation of value=20
received couldn=92t be easier.  If stock of the buyer is to be the=20
currency, the seller=92s calculation is still relatively easy: just=20
figure the market value in cash of what is to be received in=20
stock.

     Meanwhile, the buyer wishing to use his own stock as=20
currency for the purchase has no problems if the stock is selling=20
in the market at full intrinsic value.

     But suppose it is selling at only half intrinsic value.  In=20
that case, the buyer is faced with the unhappy prospect of using=20
a substantially undervalued currency to make its purchase.

     Ironically, were the buyer to instead be a seller of its=20
entire business, it too could negotiate for, and probably get,=20
full intrinsic business value.  But when the buyer makes a=20
partial sale of itself - <i>and that is what the issuance of shares=20
to make an acquisition amounts to</i> - it can customarily get no=20
higher value set on its shares than the market chooses to grant=20
it.

     The acquirer who nevertheless barges ahead ends up using an=20
undervalued (market value) currency to pay for a fully valued=20
(negotiated value) property.  In effect, the acquirer must give=20
up $2 of value to receive $1 of value.  Under such circumstances,=20
a marvelous business purchased at a fair sales price becomes a=20
terrible buy.  For gold valued as gold cannot be purchased=20
intelligently through the utilization of gold - or even silver -=20
valued as lead.

     If, however, the thirst for size and action is strong=20
enough, the acquirer=92s manager will find ample rationalizations=20
for such a value-destroying issuance of stock.  Friendly=20
investment bankers will reassure him as to the soundness of his=20
actions. (Don=92t ask the barber whether you need a haircut.)

     A few favorite rationalizations employed by stock-issuing=20
managements follow:

     (a) =93The company we=92re buying is going to be worth a lot=20
         more in the future.=94 (Presumably so is the interest in=20
         the old business that is being traded away; future=20
         prospects are implicit in the business valuation=20
         process.  If 2X is issued for X, the imbalance still=20
         exists when both parts double in business value.)

     (b) =93We have to grow.=94 (Who, it might be asked, is the =93we=94? =
=20
         For present shareholders, the reality is that all=20
         existing businesses shrink when shares are issued.  Were=20
         Berkshire to issue shares tomorrow for an acquisition,=20
         Berkshire would own everything that it now owns plus the=20
         new business, but <i>your</i> interest in such hard-to-match=20
         businesses as See=92s Candy Shops, National Indemnity,=20
         etc. would automatically be reduced.  If (1) your family=20
         owns a 120-acre farm and (2)  you invite a neighbor with=20
         60 acres of comparable land to merge his farm into an=20
         equal partnership - with you to be managing partner,=20
         then (3) your managerial domain will have grown to 180=20
         acres but you will have permanently shrunk by 25% your=20
         family=92s ownership interest in both acreage and crops. =20
         Managers who want to expand their domain at the expense=20
         of owners might better consider a career in government.)

     (c) =93Our stock is undervalued and we=92ve minimized its use in=20
         this deal - but we need to give the selling shareholders=20
         51% in stock and 49% in cash so that certain of those=20
         shareholders can get the tax-free exchange they want.=94=20
         (This argument acknowledges that it is beneficial to the=20
         acquirer to hold down the issuance of shares, and we like=20
         that.  But if it hurts the old owners to utilize shares=20
         on a 100% basis, it very likely hurts on a 51% basis. =20
         After all, a man is not charmed if a spaniel defaces his=20
         lawn, just because it=92s a spaniel and not a St. Bernard. =20
         And the wishes of sellers can=92t be the determinant of the=20
         best interests of the buyer - what would happen if,=20
         heaven forbid, the seller insisted that as a condition of=20
         merger the CEO of the acquirer be replaced?)

     There are three ways to avoid destruction of value for old=20
owners when shares are issued for acquisitions.  One is to have a=20
true business-value-for-business-value merger, such as the=20
Berkshire-Blue Chip combination is intended to be.  Such a merger=20
attempts to be fair to shareholders of <i>both</i> parties, with each=20
receiving just as much as it gives in terms of intrinsic business=20
value.  The Dart Industries-Kraft and Nabisco Standard Brands=20
mergers appeared to be of this type, but they are the exceptions. =20
It=92s not that acquirers wish to avoid such deals; it=92s just that=20
they are very hard to do.

     The second route presents itself when the acquirer=92s stock=20
sells at or above its intrinsic business value.  In that=20
situation, the use of stock as currency actually may enhance the=20
wealth of the acquiring company=92s owners.  Many mergers were=20
accomplished on this basis in the 1965-69 period.  The results=20
were the converse of most of the activity since 1970: the=20
shareholders of the <i>acquired</i> company received very inflated=20
currency (frequently pumped up by dubious accounting and=20
promotional techniques) and were the losers of wealth through=20
such transactions.

     During recent years the second solution has been available=20
to very few large companies.  The exceptions have primarily been=20
those companies in glamorous or promotional businesses to which=20
the market temporarily attaches valuations at or above intrinsic=20
business valuation.

     The third solution is for the acquirer to go ahead with the=20
acquisition, but then subsequently repurchase a quantity of=20
shares equal to the number issued in the merger.  In this manner,=20
what originally was a stock-for-stock merger can be converted,=20
effectively, into a cash-for-stock acquisition.  Repurchases of=20
this kind are damage-repair moves.  Regular readers will=20
correctly guess that we much prefer repurchases that directly=20
enhance the wealth of owners instead of repurchases that merely=20
repair previous damage.  Scoring touchdowns is more exhilarating=20
than recovering one=92s fumbles.  But, when a fumble has occurred,=20
recovery is important and we heartily recommend damage-repair=20
repurchases that turn a bad stock deal into a fair cash deal.

     The language utilized in mergers tends to confuse the issues=20
and encourage irrational actions by managers.  For example,=20
=93dilution=94 is usually carefully calculated on a pro forma basis=20
for both book value and current earnings per share.  Particular=20
emphasis is given to the latter item.  When that calculation is=20
negative (dilutive) from the acquiring company=92s standpoint, a=20
justifying explanation will be made (internally, if not=20
elsewhere) that the lines will cross favorably at some point in=20
the future. (While deals often fail in practice, they never fail=20
in projections - if the CEO is visibly panting over a prospective=20
acquisition, subordinates and consultants will supply the=20
requisite projections to rationalize any price.) Should the=20
calculation produce numbers that are immediately positive - that=20
is, anti-dilutive - for the acquirer, no comment is thought to be=20
necessary.

     The attention given this form of dilution is overdone:=20
current earnings per share (or even earnings per share of the=20
next few years) are an important variable in most business=20
valuations, but far from all powerful.

     There have been plenty of mergers, non-dilutive in this=20
limited sense, that were instantly value destroying for the=20
acquirer.  And some mergers that have diluted current and near-
term earnings per share have in fact been value-enhancing.  What=20
really counts is whether a merger is dilutive or anti-dilutive in=20
terms of intrinsic business value (a judgment involving=20
consideration of many variables).  We believe calculation of=20
dilution from this viewpoint to be all-important (and too seldom=20
made).

     A second language problem relates to the equation of=20
exchange.  If Company A announces that it will issue shares to=20
merge with Company B, the process is customarily described as=20
=93Company A to Acquire Company B=94, or =93B Sells to A=94.  Clearer=20
thinking about the matter would result if a more awkward but more=20
accurate description were used: =93Part of A sold to acquire B=94, or=20
=93Owners of B to receive part of A in exchange for their=20
properties=94.  In a trade, what you are giving is just as=20
important as what you are getting.  This remains true even when=20
the final tally on what is being given is delayed.  Subsequent=20
sales of common stock or convertible issues, either to complete=20
the financing for a deal or to restore balance sheet strength,=20
must be fully counted in evaluating the fundamental mathematics=20
of the original acquisition. (If corporate pregnancy is going to=20
be the consequence of corporate mating, the time to face that=20
fact is before the moment of ecstasy.)

     Managers and directors might sharpen their thinking by=20
asking themselves if they would sell 100% of their business on=20
the same basis they are being asked to sell part of it.  And if=20
it isn=92t smart to sell all on such a basis, they should ask=20
themselves why it is smart to sell a portion.  A cumulation of=20
small managerial stupidities will produce a major stupidity - not=20
a major triumph. (Las Vegas has been built upon the wealth=20
transfers that occur when people engage in seemingly-small=20
disadvantageous capital transactions.)

     The =93giving versus getting=94 factor can most easily be=20
calculated in the case of registered investment companies. =20
Assume Investment Company X, selling at 50% of asset value,=20
wishes to merge with Investment Company Y.  Assume, also, that=20
Company X therefore decides to issue shares equal in market value=20
to 100% of Y=92s asset value.

     Such a share exchange would leave X trading $2 of its=20
previous intrinsic value for $1 of Y=92s intrinsic value.  Protests=20
would promptly come forth from both X=92s shareholders and the SEC,=20
which rules on the fairness of registered investment company=20
mergers.  Such a transaction simply would not be allowed.

     In the case of manufacturing, service, financial companies,=20
etc., values are not normally as precisely calculable as in the=20
case of investment companies.  But we have seen mergers in these=20
industries that just as dramatically destroyed value for the=20
owners of the acquiring company as was the case in the=20
hypothetical illustration above.  This destruction could not=20
happen if management and directors would assess the fairness of=20
any transaction by using the same yardstick in the measurement of=20
both businesses.

     Finally, a word should be said about the =93double whammy=94=20
effect upon owners of the acquiring company when value-diluting=20
stock issuances occur.  Under such circumstances, the first blow=20
is the loss of intrinsic business value that occurs through the=20
merger itself.  The second is the downward revision in market=20
valuation that, quite rationally, is given to that now-diluted=20
business value.  For current and prospective owners=20
understandably will not pay as much for assets lodged in the=20
hands of a management that has a record of wealth-destruction=20
through unintelligent share issuances as they will pay for assets=20
entrusted to a management with precisely equal operating talents,=20
but a known distaste for anti-owner actions.  Once management=20
shows itself insensitive to the interests of owners, shareholders=20
will suffer a long time from the price/value ratio afforded their=20
stock (relative to other stocks), no matter what assurances=20
management gives that the value-diluting action taken was a one-
of-a-kind event.

     Those assurances are treated by the market much as one-bug-
in-the-salad explanations are treated at restaurants.  Such=20
explanations, even when accompanied by a new waiter, do not=20
eliminate a drop in the demand (and hence market value) for=20
salads, both on the part of the offended customer and his=20
neighbors pondering what to order.  Other things being equal, the=20
highest stock market prices relative to intrinsic business value=20
are given to companies whose managers have demonstrated their=20
unwillingness to issue shares at any time on terms unfavorable to=20
the owners of the business.

     At Berkshire, or any company whose policies we determine=20
(including Blue Chip and Wesco), we will issue shares only if our=20
owners receive in business value as much as we give.  We will not=20
equate activity with progress or corporate size with owner-
wealth.


<i>Miscellaneous</i>

     This annual report is read by a varied audience, and it is=20
possible that some members of that audience may be helpful to us=20
in our acquisition program.

     We prefer:

        (1) large purchases (at least $5 million of after-tax=20
            earnings),

        (2) demonstrated consistent earning power (future=20
            projections are of little interest to us, nor are=20
            =93turn-around=94 situations),

        (3) businesses earning good returns on equity while=20
            employing little or no debt,

        (4) management in place (we can=92t supply it),

        (5) simple businesses (if there=92s lots of technology, we=20
            won=92t understand it),

        (6) an offering price (we don=92t want to waste our time or=20
            that of the seller by talking, even preliminarily,=20
            about a transaction when price is unknown).

     We will not engage in unfriendly transactions.  We can=20
promise complete confidentiality and a very fast answer as to=20
possible interest - customarily within five minutes.  Cash=20
purchases are preferred, but we will consider the use of stock=20
when it can be done on the basis described in the previous=20
section.

